title: Labour Chooses Kinnock
tags: event
view-if: con_in_government = 1 and second_term = 1 and lab_leader == "Foot" and time - second_term_start >= 3
max-visits: 1
new-page: true
on-arrival: {!
Q.lab_leader = "Kinnock";
Q.lab_leader_rating = 46;
Q.lab_left = 0;
Q.labour_credibility += 8;
Q.labour_civil_war -= 10;
// a lasting recovery: Labour's voters start to come home
var back = {ab: 0.5, c1: 1, c2: 2, de: 1.5};
for (var c in back) {
    Q.baseline_support[c + '_lab'] += back[c];
    Q[c + '_lab'] += back[c];
}
!}
face-image: img/events/foot.jpg

= Labour Chooses Kinnock

Michael Foot has stood down after the election, and Labour has chosen Neil Kinnock, a 41-year-old Welsh left-winger who has never held office, with seven-tenths of the vote in its electoral college. Roy Hattersley, from the party's right, is his deputy, and the papers call it the dream ticket. Kinnock is quick, funny and young, and he wants to make Labour electable again.

#################### 1983-84

@parkinson
title: The Parkinson Affair
tags: event
view-if: con_in_government = 1 and second_term = 1 and parkinson_seen = 0 and time - second_term_start >= 3
max-visits: 1
new-page: true
on-arrival: parkinson_seen = 1
face-image: img/cards/parkinson.jpg

= The Parkinson Affair

Cecil Parkinson, who ran the election campaign and now runs Trade and Industry, has admitted that his former secretary is expecting his child. Now, in the middle of the party conference, she has given her own account to The Times, and the story is on every front page.

- @parkinson_go: Accept his resignation.
- @parkinson_stay: Stand by him.

@parkinson_go
subtitle: The story dies quickly. You lose a loyal ally.
on-arrival: parkinson_resigned = 1; thatcher_rating -= 1; loyalist_dissent += 2

Parkinson resigns before the conference ends. You make it clear that you hope to bring him back one day. Norman Tebbit takes over at Trade and Industry.

@parkinson_stay
subtitle: Loyalty, at a price: the story runs and runs.
on-arrival: thatcher_rating -= 4; loyalist_dissent -= 2; right_dissent += 3; rebellion += 1

Parkinson stays. The story runs for weeks, the moralists on the backbenches grumble, and every interview you give comes back to it.

@gchq
title: GCHQ
tags: event
view-if: con_in_government = 1 and second_term = 1 and gchq_seen = 0 and time >= 73
max-visits: 1
new-page: true
on-arrival: gchq_seen = 1
face-image: img/events/westminster.jpg

= GCHQ

The intelligence agencies want trade unions banned at GCHQ, the listening station in Cheltenham, after strikes there during the civil service dispute of 1981. Staff would be offered £1,000 each to give up their membership.

- @ban_unions: Ban the unions at GCHQ.
- @leave_unions: Leave them alone.

@ban_unions
subtitle: The right approves. The TUC is outraged.
on-arrival: gchq_ban = 1; tuc_mood += 10; union_power -= 1; right_dissent -= 2; wet_dissent += 2; lib_relation -= 2

The ban is announced. The TUC calls it an attack on a basic freedom and holds a day of protest, and a few hundred staff refuse to give up their cards.

@leave_unions
subtitle: The TUC notices. The security services grumble.
on-arrival: tuc_mood -= 4; right_dissent += 2

The unions stay. The agencies get a no-strike agreement instead, after months of argument.

@brighton
title: Brighton
tags: event
view-if: con_in_government = 1 and second_term = 1 and brighton_seen = 0 and time >= 82 and month = 10
max-visits: 1
new-page: true
on-arrival: brighton_seen = 1; thatcher_rating += 5; right_dissent -= 3; wet_dissent -= 3; uup_relation += 5; c1_con += 0.5; c2_con += 0.5; de_con += 0.5
face-image: img/events/thatcher_1980.jpg

= Brighton

At ten to three in the morning, an IRA bomb hidden in the Grand Hotel in Brighton explodes during the party conference. Five people are killed, and Norman Tebbit and many others are pulled from the rubble. You were working on your speech in a room that survived.

At half past nine that morning the conference opens on time. You tell it that the bomb was an attempt to cripple the government, and that the fact that we are gathered here, shocked but composed and determined, is a sign that the attack has failed.

@hong_kong_declaration
title: The Future of Hong Kong
tags: event
view-if: con_in_government = 1 and second_term = 1 and hong_kong_declaration = 0 and time >= 83
max-visits: 1
new-page: true
on-arrival: hong_kong_declaration = 1
face-image: img/events/westminster.jpg

= The Future of Hong Kong

[? if hong_kong_firm = 1 : Two years of hard bargaining since your visit to Beijing have got us as far as they can. ?][? if hong_kong_firm = 0 : Two years of talks since your visit to Beijing have produced a draft agreement. ?] The lease on the New Territories runs out in 1997, and without them Hong Kong is not viable. China will take sovereignty back; in return it promises that Hong Kong's way of life will continue for fifty years: "one country, two systems".

- @sign_declaration: Sign the Joint Declaration in Beijing.
- @hold_out: Hold out for continued British administration.

@sign_declaration
subtitle: The Americans approve. The right grumbles about a sell-out.
on-arrival: us_relation += 3; europe_relation += 2; right_dissent += 2; wet_dissent -= 2

You sign the Joint Declaration in the Great Hall of the People. The people of Hong Kong were not asked, and many of them begin to look for passports elsewhere.

@hold_out
subtitle: The right cheers. Beijing threatens to act alone.
on-arrival: right_dissent -= 3; us_relation -= 2; thatcher_rating -= 1; economic_growth -= 0.1

The talks stall. Beijing announces that it will settle Hong Kong's future by itself if need be, and the Hang Seng index falls by a quarter.

#################### 1985

@sterling_1985
title: The Pound at a Dollar
tags: event
view-if: con_in_government = 1 and second_term = 1 and sterling_1985_seen = 0 and time >= 85
max-visits: 1
new-page: true
on-arrival: sterling_1985_seen = 1; sterling -= 6
face-image: img/events/bank_1983.jpg

= The Pound at a Dollar

A strong dollar and a falling oil price have sent sterling down to $1.10, and the markets think it is going to parity. A remark from your press office, that the government won't defend any particular rate, has made it worse.

- @defend_pound: Raise Bank Rate to defend the pound.
- @let_it_fall: Let the pound find its level.

@defend_pound
subtitle: Mortgage holders pay. The pound recovers.
on-arrival: interest_rate += 3; inflation -= 0.3; sterling += 6; c1_con -= 1; c2_con -= 1; dry_dissent -= 1

Bank Rate goes up by three points in a week, to 14%. The pound steadies, and mortgage rates follow Bank Rate up.

@let_it_fall
subtitle: Exporters cheer. Prices rise, and the papers talk of a humiliation.
on-arrival: inflation += 1; sterling -= 4; economic_growth += 0.2; thatcher_rating -= 2; dry_dissent += 2

The pound touches $1.05. Exporters in the Midlands find their order books filling, and the Treasury finds the price of imports rising.

@bournemouth
title: Bournemouth
tags: event
